WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.001
McCarthy required

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.035
the better part of a week

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.503
to grind down the conservative

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.372
opposition to his speaker bid

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:06.740
and the way things have been done

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.041
in the House for decades.

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:08.842
One of the holdouts,

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:09.743
or Republican Congressman

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:10.910
Chip Roy of Texas,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.013
was instrumental in delivering him

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:13.913
the gavel.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.482
Roy negotiated a laundry list

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.116
of concessions

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.951
from McCarthy designed to spread power

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.619
among the House rank and file

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:20.387
and particularly

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:21.721
the House Freedom Caucus.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:22.455
And Roy's deal

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.624
ultimately flipped up to 15 votes

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:25.392
for McCarthy,

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:27.460
paving the way for his victory

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:27.994
Friday night.

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.662
Joining me now for his first interview

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:30.430
after the speaker

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.432
election is Texas Republican Congressman

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:32.966
Chip Roy.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:34.801
First of all, congratulations

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.302
on everything that happened

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.038
and getting what you wanted

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:38.972
in the negotiations.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.673
It's not it's not necessarily

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.609
a sure thing that that that that happens.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:44.377
We are

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:45.812
just learning some

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.547
of the promises McCarthy made,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.215
capping spending at 20,

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.418
22 levels for fiscal year 2024,

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:53.553
a subcommittee to investigate

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:54.921
the Justice Department,

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:55.922
more Freedom Caucus

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.390
representation on important committees

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.591
like the Rules Committee,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.793
a one member threshold

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.061
for a motion to vacate,

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:02.462
which is what it used to be before 20.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.196
19.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:04.931
What other commitments did McCarthy make

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.266
you can tell us about?

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.434
Well, first of all,

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:07.667
you know,

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:08.768
let's remember that

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:11.604
a little temporary conflict is necessary

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:12.138
in this town

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.240
in order to stop this town

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.608
from rolling over to the American people.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.177
I don't think anybody

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:17.577
on either

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.245
side of the aisle could say

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.080
with a straight face

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.549
that they think that Washington is doing

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.116
good work

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:23.850
for the American people

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.586
on a regular basis and isn't broken.

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.321
We have to work to fix this place.

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:28.822
And look,

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:30.957
some of the tensions you saw on display

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.560
when we saw some of the interactions

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.129
there between Mike Rogers and Matt Gates.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:36.529
You know,

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.565
some of that is we need a little of that.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:39.632
We need a little of this

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.067
sort of breaking the glass

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.569
in order to get us to the table,

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:43.203
in order to fight

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.003
for the American people

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.038
and to change the way

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:47.173
this place is dysfunctional.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.708
So this all started going back

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:49.142
last summer.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:51.177
We wanted rules to open this place up.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.712
We wanted more transparency.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:53.780
We wanted more openness,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.348
more ability to add

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:56.249
amendments to the force.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.150
For example, you ask,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.518
what else did we get?

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.087
We got amendments to be able to ensure

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.055
we got the ability

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:02.922
to offer amendments

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:03.890
on the floor of the House

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.825
during appropriations

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.260
that will open it up again.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.094
We haven't done that.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:09.362
So since I've been in Congress,

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:09.963
I've not been able

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.598
to offer an amendment on the floor.

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:12.832
There hasn't been an amendment

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:15.001
offered an open debate since May of 2016.

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:15.902
Is it if we just roll

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:17.537
like anyone can offer an amendment?

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:19.739
Absolutely. Within appropriations.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:20.974
We will also be striving

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:22.642
for more open rules.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.844
We put more conservatives,

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:26.146
some Freedom Caucus members

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:27.714
and we're still working through

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.249
who those will be on the Rules

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:30.683
Committee, the powerful Rules Committee,

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.152
which is the funnel

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:33.153
by which legislation

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.921
gets to the floor of the House.

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:35.555
But importantly,

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.623
we were trying to stand up

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.024
for rank and file members

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:38.591
because too

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.960
often and we saw this in December.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.729
Too often bills are cooked up

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:43.897
with a handful of people.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:44.864
They're brought through the Rules

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:45.932
Committee jammed through,

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:46.699
put on the floor,

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:48.368
and you have to vote yes or no.

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.003
The American people are tired of that.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:50.637
We need to be able

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:51.571
to see some of the stuff

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.539
we got to see this week.

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.373
You and I were talking

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.641
ahead of time cameras

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.342
because C-SPAN controlled the cameras.

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:57.043
Are you in favor of that?

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.745
Because I love the C-SPAN cameras.

00:02:58.745 --> 00:02:58.978
Look,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.747
I think drawing the American people

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:01.648
into the conversation

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:02.682
is the debate on the floor.

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.283
I mean, if you're going

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:04.617
to have cameras there,

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:05.952
well, let's look at the action.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.387
Let's see if you're in favor

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:08.555
of transparency.

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:10.890
C-SPAN gets to control it. Well, I would.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.458
Let me go look into the ins

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.293
and outs of all of that.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.060
But I think it is

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.795
what the American people were able to see

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:16.296
unfold on

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:16.696
the floor

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.364
was a good thing for our democracy

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.532
and our republic. Right.

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.700
It was a good thing for people

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.802
to be able to see the inner workings.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.071
And this isn't just the shirts and skins

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:27.440
red and blue, too, team thing.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.209
This is history

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.310
because this hasn't happened

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.778
in the last hundred years.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.713
But understand why that so

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.515
to party entrenchment

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:36.349
has made it to where

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.850
we don't have a good back and forth

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:38.885
to sit at the table

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:39.886
and try to accomplish this.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:42.121
So I'm all in favor of transparency.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.557
I'm a journalist. I want everybody out.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:45.692
So I hope you'll look into

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:46.492
the C-SPAN thing

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:46.893
because

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.461
because I think that really does show

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:48.962
the American people.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:50.396
I don't think it's a bad thing to show

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:51.631
Democrats and Republicans

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.600
tough negotiations, etc.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.602
Will in the name of transparency.

00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:57.870
Will you or the Speaker

00:03:58.037 --> 00:03:59.072
put out a list

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.540
of the concessions of these changes?

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.008
Because they're not all, as you know,

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:03.476
they're not all reflected

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:04.244
in the rules

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:06.246
that are going to come up for a vote

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:07.614
this week. Sure.

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:07.981
I mean,

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:09.849
and again, part of this is all sensitive,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:10.950
right, as you're kind of going through it

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.185
and kind of holding everybody together.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.420
We got to get through the rules on Monday

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.021
and we're going through

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:15.922
the steering process

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:16.990
of getting folks on committees.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.291
But but for example,

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:20.994
when we say we want it, we put out a list

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.629
in, what, December 8th

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.030
try saying, hey, here's what we think.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.665
We need to transform this place.

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:26.699
Here's what we need to see

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:27.900
in any speaker.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.801
And so we've been out

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.003
and open about that.

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:30.903
But I want to make clear,

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.705
this isn't about trying to chip

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.407
Roy once a spot on a committee.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:35.642
I haven't been promised anything.

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:37.543
Literally, I've got nothing out of this

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.778
deal for me personally.

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:39.279
Does that mean

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.713
I might end up on Rules Committee?

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:41.381
Maybe,

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:43.449
if that's what the my colleagues want

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.418
We still have to go through that process.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:46.119
But what we have

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.321
extracted is promises from the speaker

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:49.255
to make sure

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:50.923
that we have ideological diversity

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.058
and representation

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.661
among these committees. Appropriations.

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:55.595
There are two Freedom

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:57.230
Caucus members out of 33

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.531
that are likely to be in the majority.

00:04:58.531 --> 00:04:59.599
So think about that.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.100
HFC is 20% of the body

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.802
at two of 33 appropriators.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:03.870
The rules committee.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.704
It's always a struggle

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:05.738
to get anybody on there

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.140
who's going to push back in the rules

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:07.874
Committee about

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:09.175
wait why are we jamming a bill

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.510
through that nobody's read right.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.945
So let's let's get representation

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:13.880
on these committees

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.714
and and

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:16.816
it's not about petty personal desires.

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:17.784
I don't want to be on the rules

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:18.117
committee.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:19.952
I want to leave my family on Sunday night

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.888
and miss my kids to come up here.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:22.722
But I might do it

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:24.223
if that's what my colleagues decide.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.592
So let me ask you a question

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.494
about whether or not the

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:29.529
whatever you want to call it, the

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:30.863
chaos, the back and forth,

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:32.832
whether or not this is going

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:33.333
to mark

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:35.068
what the Congress is going to look like

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.003
going forward over the next two years.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.104
McCarthy promised

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:38.971
one of the promises

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.740
was not to raise the debt ceiling

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.309
without some accompanying spending cuts.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:43.710
Correct.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.812
Congress had a similar showdown

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:47.980
trying to force spending cuts in 2011.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:49.449
It led to America's credit

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:50.717
rating getting downgraded

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:51.951
because the

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:52.719
because they weren't able

00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:54.687
to arrive at the deal

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.022
the markets felt the U.S.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:56.823
government had become

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.624
according to the downgrade

00:05:58.624 --> 00:05:59.258
and quote

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:00.626
less stable, less effective,

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:02.495
less predictable

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:03.763
and that had a real tangible

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:04.797
detrimental effect

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:05.698
on the American people

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.666
and their pocketbooks,

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.067
their retirement savings.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:08.701
What's the plan

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.770
to make sure that doesn't happen again

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.571
that the chaos

00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:12.805
we saw this week or whatever you

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:13.339
want to call it

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.008
doesn't become

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.910
just what happens every time,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.746
especially when we're talking about

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:21.981
major must-pass legislation

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:23.282
like raising the debt ceiling,

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.852
which is just about paying the bills

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.687
for what has already been paid.

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:29.822
Well, the fastest way to guarantee

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:30.690
that we have debt

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.492
rating problems is to keep spending money

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:34.460
we don't have and keep piling up debt.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:35.795
And that's what we're doing.

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.430
The unique party in Washington,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:38.064
Republicans

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:39.699
and Democrats, the powerbrokers,

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:40.500
which, by the way,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.868
were reflected by Mike Rogers

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:43.569
when he goes in and he's pushing

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.771
back on the bill, says,

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:46.139
I will finish you. Right.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.440
So why is that?

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.275
Because when you push back on the swamp,

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.043
the swamp is going to push right back.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:52.011
We saw that in display.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:52.678
That's okay.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:53.613
So you said, well,

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:54.180
are we going to have

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.048
this kind of conflict going forward?

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:56.916
I hope so.

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:57.784
But we started this.

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:59.852
Now it's January.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:01.587
So the debt ceiling debate

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:02.655
is going to be in a few months.

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:03.956
Let's do it now, guys.

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:04.991
Let's get this out there.

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.692
Both sides of the aisle,

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:08.161
everybody within each party.

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:08.961
Let's figure out

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:10.396
how we're going to actually fix this,

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:10.997
because the American

00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:12.732
people are sick of us not doing our job.

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:14.734
There is a bigger risk, though,

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:15.334
for the U.S.

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:17.203
defaulting on the debt ceiling

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:18.271
when it comes to the debt ceiling vote.

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:19.739
Then some short

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.107
term embarrassment

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:22.542
for Kevin McCarthy, that's

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:24.444
the default is a bigger deal

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:25.912
and could actually cost

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:27.480
